A||||9||BAM-BAM||What do I need to make a 3.4 TDC work in my 85GT????||||12||
Z||000000||BAM-BAM||04-12-2001||08:53 PM|||| As you all may or may not know Kerry (Owenslee) has a 3.4 TDC for sale and I am going to buy it, provided I have all my eggs in one basket. He has just the engine. Besides the obvious...ECM, harness, what do I need  to make this work? What type of transmission do I need? What type of mounts, adapters, so on and so forth are required? What about rewire schematics? Axels, Air box, throttle cabels, trans cabels, Pumps, pullys, Duct tape, anything? I would like to get this phase of my project done. I've just about had it with the 3800SC engine swap. I just can't seem to get the main aspect correct, The engine!!!! Give some opinions and advise. Is it worth it, should I hold out for the SC, I"M SO CONFUSED!!!<P>Ouch, I got the brain pain, OUT!!!!<P>------------------<BR>Knowledge is POWER!<BR>That is why the ignorant remain Weak!||||reg||12
Z||000001||spiz||04-12-2001||09:33 PM||||I am no expert. I just started this swap myself but from what I understand the biggest hassle is the wiring and the speed sensor. I think Chris West will make a wiring harness from the fiero harness and the 3.4 harness for around 250.00. there is a guy named Micahle Smith who has a pretty good web page. http/fiero.cc/fiero-tdc/members/mws  I think thats right. do a search here there has been a lot of discussion on this subject. By the way I think Michael has his car for sale. Asking 6000.00. If you are going to use the 460te trans I have one I will let go cheap.||||reg||1
Z||000002||87GTZ34||04-12-2001||10:32 PM||||BB I have a '96 (hence the name) engine getting prepped at the engine shop now.  I have had it for about 1.5 years and haven't made the time yet so I decided to start a little while ago. No one (yet) has a step by step manual for the conversion although CW has pieces on the menu (but your order may take awhile).  I modified my own front eng mount and the rest of the mounts are stock GM from a pont 6000 with a 4T60E.  Depending on the year of the electronics, you can only run the auto.  If you get the auto get one with gearing for the 3.4 TDC/DOHC as that eng needs to wind up.  I got one from a '96 MC and has the designation PB on the ID stamp.  If you mate a 3.1 or 3800 (which were geared higher) you will be disappointed with low end torque.  If you use non OBDII electronics, Terry has some experience with needed computer mods.  I am using the '96 electronics including cruise and retaining all '96 emissions equip to 'keep it green'. I am also setting mine up for a turbo (lowering compression and getting forged pistons made from JE).  <BR>carcas mods - the hinge on the passenger side needs to modified, some/most people chop it in half but I am 'sliding mine over 4", modifying the hinge and using gas struts to hold the lid up.  The upper dogbone strut bracket has to be removed (yes that means drilling the body welds) that wasn't as bad as I thought it would be.  And last but certainly not least is the exhaust system, ain't been there haven't done that (yet).||||reg||1
Z||000003||Philphine||04-12-2001||11:40 PM||||so if i were to try this swap from a 4 cyl. and had acess to a pontiac 6000 ('86 ste), i should get the engine mounts? i know thats a stumbling block with a 4 to 6cly swap.||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||04-13-2001||12:25 AM||||I've done both of these swaps. A 94 DOHC and a 92 SC. If I do it again, I won't do a DOHC. DOHC into an 85GT and SC into an 86SE. Same car.<P>Both have the exhaust issue. On these, we made custom crossover pipes like the Fiero V6 crossover and used stock exhaust routing. Otherwise, you are stuck with Supertraps or the like. Not me jack.<P>The wiring is about the same work. Figure 20-30 hours. <P>Both lose the trunk springs. The DOHC needs a bit of trimming on hood, the 92SC need alot.<P>The DOHC fits on the Getrag. The SC needs to be super-glued to it.<P>DOHC and manual trans uses stock axles. The SC with an automatic needs custom axles.<P>Both need custom thermostat housing, cooling and heat hoses.<P>The DOHC sucks for mounting a dogbone, the SC's are easier.<P>These engine weren't OBDII (non-flash), so I could hack the code and fix the speedo in the ECM. <P>Both need the dogbone mount on the trunk removed. <P>The DOHC is a tighter fit than the SC. DOHC's are more cantankerous. Sounds great.<P>A 95+ DOHC and 96+ SC would be slightly different, but 95% the same.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 04-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||BAM-BAM||04-13-2001||12:56 AM|||| So Terry, What you are saying is that either way I go I'll have combative issues? What kind of tranny does the DOHC use? Can I use my stock auto? Is it even worth the time? I really need some definitive answers. I want something more than the standard 3.4L swap. Terry E-Mail me with your thoughts as I don't want to waste any more space than neccessary. <P>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Knowledge is POWER!<BR>That is why the ignorant remain Weak!||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||04-13-2001||01:09 AM||||You have mail.<P>--------------------------------------------<BR>"I hope the meek inherit the earth so I can knock 'em down and take it."||||reg||1
Z||000007||Black88GT||04-13-2001||01:10 AM||||terry, by that post it seems you make it out that the 3.4 DOHC is a better swap, yet you say that you wouldn't do it again if you had to choose between a 3.8 SC and a 3.4 DOHC.  Could you explain a little more?  I like the fact that the 3.4 DOHC uses stock axles on manual tranny.  It seems to me if you want an auto, go for the 3.8 SC and if you want a manual, go 3.4 DOHC.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD - MAFOA<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://88database.homestead.com/files/AF.gif">    <BR><I>"Once you go black, you never go back..."</I><BR><I>"The Customer Can Have Any Color He Wants So Long As It's Black" </I> -Henry Ford||||reg||5
Z||000008||terryk||04-13-2001||11:57 AM||||I think it's a matter of preference in some areas. For me, the DOHC is just not enough umph for the effort considering my goal which is street light tangos. No, I'm not encouraging drag racing in front of elementary schools so don't start....<P>The DOHC is a high winder and does match the *spirit* of the Fiero. No low-end torque at all though. Comes alive at 3000 and hang on.<P>The SC on the other hand really does what most of us want, the neck-breaking torque of a big-block muscle car. <P>The question is, is the Fiero a sports car or a muscle car? Beats me.<P>Buttttttttt......., if I was bent on a manual trans, the DOHC shoots to the top of the list since I wouldn't put an SC on a Getrag, but that's just me.<P>The Buick fit is better than the DOHC overall.||||reg||1
